# Piper TTS behind the repo's TTS protocol port (8898). CPU, on every architecture.
#
# SAME ENGINE AS THE MAC, NOT A WYOMING CLIENT. The trainer no longer speaks
# Wyoming: it speaks the TTS protocol (tts-service/tts_protocol/), and both the
# Docker image and the Mac's engine project (tts-service/engines/piper) run the
# SAME in-process piper-tts code behind it. One implementation to audit, one
# G2P to pin, and the measured behaviour transfers across machines.
#
# THIS USED TO BE A WYOMING CLIENT, AND THE HISTORY IS WHY IT IS NOT.
#
# The image was `FROM rhasspy/wyoming-piper:2.4.3` (a CPU base, chosen after the
# CUDA A/B - it was once built on nvidia/cuda with onnxruntime-gpu, and
# --use-cuda measured 2.5x SLOWER because wyoming-piper starts a fresh session
# per request, so every render paid CUDA context setup on a model small enough
# that the setup dominates). The published image was the right pick for the
# CLIENT it served at the time - multi-arch, pinned, voices on demand - but the
# protocol split made the client irrelevant: what this image needs now is an
# engine that can host the protocol, and the in-process one IS the engine.
#
# PINNED, not floating - the same discipline the old header applied to the
# base tag, one level deeper. piper-tts 1.7.0 is the version the old
# wyoming-piper:2.4.3 image carried in its /usr/src/.venv (verified 2026-09-07
# by running the image); a fresh `pip install piper-tts` today resolves 1.8.0 -
# a newer G2P, which changes what the voices say. The exclusion lists in
# train/corpus/piper.py were audited against 1.7.0, so bumping this MUST happen
# together with tts-service/engines/piper/pyproject.toml (the Mac's copy of the
# same pin) and scripts/start-piper-host.sh, with a re-audit. onnxruntime is
# pinned to the version 1.7.0 resolves, so the inference runtime matches what
# was measured.
#
# THE BASE IS THE ONLY FLOATING THING, AND IT IS A STABLE ONE: python:3.12-slim
# publishes linux/amd64 and linux/arm64, so this runs native on a Mac (which is
# where the eval corpus most needs to be generatable) as well as on the box.
# The old header's warning about amd64-only bases running the whole corpus
# stage under silent qemu emulation still applies - keep it multi-arch.
FROM python:3.12-slim

# Everything in one layer: the G2P + inference stack, then the two repo
# packages (the protocol, then the engine). --no-deps on the repo packages:
# their only dependencies are the four pip packages installed just above, and
# resolving them from PyPI here would re-fetch versions this file just pinned.
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir \
        numpy scipy \
        piper-tts==1.7.0 \
        onnxruntime==1.29.0

COPY tts-service/tts_protocol/ /tmp/tts_protocol/
COPY tts-service/engines/piper/ /tmp/piper_engine/
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir --no-deps /tmp/tts_protocol /tmp/piper_engine \
    && rm -rf /tmp/tts_protocol /tmp/piper_engine

# Voices are downloaded on demand into --voices and get large once a
# multi-speaker model like libritts_r is pulled - it carries 904 speakers - so
# this wants to be a mounted volume rather than image layers.
VOLUME ["/data"]
EXPOSE 8898

# One voice loaded at a time, serial by design: the protocol server holds a
# single lock (tts_protocol/server.py), and the voice-outer order of
# train/corpus/piper.py is a correctness property on top of that.
CMD ["python", "-m", "piper_engine", "--host", "0.0.0.0", "--port", "8898", \
     "--voices", "/data"]
